  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Alabama received $11.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $8.1B, making defense work the dominant source of federal spending in the state. Award activity shows 81,432 awards with an average award size of $140K. Obligations fell 37.2% year over year. Federal buying is concentrated by agency and vendor: NASA obligated $1.8B, and THE BOEING COMPANY, LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION, and DYNETICS, INC. are the top three vendors by total obligations. Key industries include Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $3.3B and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $2B. Ship Building and Repairing and Engineering Services also rank in the top five industries. These figures reflect partial fiscal-year totals and contract obligations only. Contractors and acquisition planners should treat the FY2026 totals as year-to-date activity rather than full-year results.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the year-over-year decline in obligations?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 37.2% year over year. The Department of Defense remains the largest buyer but obligated $8.1B, which is only a portion of the state's total $11.4B in FY2026 to date. Other large buyers, like the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, obligated $1.8B, but combined agency totals are lower than prior full-year peaks. The FY2026 totals are partial fiscal-year figures, which contributes to the year-over-year drop."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries dominate Alabama contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buying agency, obligating $8.1B. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration is the second largest buyer at $1.8B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $407M. THE BOEING COMPANY, LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION, and DYNETICS, INC. are the top three vendors at $1.2B, $1.1B, and $1B respectively, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry at $3.3B."
      }
    ]
  },
